#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# level

#% text
# `level(x, levels)` gives the list of the parts of `x` at the levels named: `n` for levels 1 to n, `[n]` for level n only, `[m, n]` for m to n.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Level 1 is the arguments of `x`, level 2 their arguments, and so on; level 0 is `x` itself.

#% item
# A negative level counts from the leaves: `[-1]` is the atoms, `[-2]` the parts of depth 2.

#% item
# Parts come in the order a depth-first walk finishes them, inner parts first; heads are not parts.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
level([[a, b], c], [2])
#% output
#= [a, b]

#% cell
level([[a, b], c], 1)
#% output
#= [[a, b], c]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Every level, and the leaves:

#% cell
level(f(g(x), y), inf)
#% output
#= [x, g(x), y]

#% cell
level(f(g(x), y), [-1])
#% output
#= [x, y]

#% text
# The values of an association are its parts:

#% cell
level(assoc(a -> x, b -> [y]), [-1])
#% output
#= [x, y]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The parts at level 1 are the arguments:

#% cell
level(f(a, b, c), [1]) == [a, b, c]
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `depth`, `map`, `apply`, `count`, `position`
